COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: cat .git-blame-ignore-revs; git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' | head -100; git ls-files | wc -l; git log --oneline | wc -l
 - Bash: run(){ label=$1; shift; echo "== $label"; for f in $(git ls-files | grep -E '\.(go|mod|md|yml)$|go.sum'); do git blame --line-porcelain $@ -- "$f" | grep '^author ' ; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | awk '{t+=$1; a[NR]=$0} END{for(i=1;i<=NR;i++){split(a[i],p," "); printf "%s  %.1f%%\n", a[i], p[1]*100/t}; print "total",t}'; }
run "default+ignore-revs, -w -M -C" -w -M -C --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs
run "go files only" -w -M -C --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs >/dev/null
echo; echo "== .go non-test only"
for f in $(git ls-files '*.go'); do git blame --line-porcelain -w -M -C --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs -- "$f" | grep '^author '; done | sort | uniq -c | so
FINAL ANSWER:
Not a one-person project, but the original author holds about half the code.

I ran `git blame` on every tracked file, with whitespace changes and moved lines followed. I skipped the `interface{}` → `any` commit listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`. There are 2,064 live lines.

| Rank | Author | Live lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Fatih Arslan | 1,125 | 54.5% |
| 2 | Linda_pp | 136 | 6.6% |
| 3 | Raj Patel | 127 | 6.2% |
| 4 | Lena Hoffmann | 103 | 5.0% |
| 5 | Matthew Hall | 98 | 4.7% |

- **Go source only:** If you count only the `.go` files (about 1,780 lines), Fatih Arslan holds 893 lines, or about 50%. Linda_pp is still second with 136 lines, about 7.6%.
- **Long tail:** About 40 people have at least one live line. The next five authors together hold about 27%.
- **Commits:** Fatih Arslan has 201 of the 300 commits. Dependabot has 38.
- **Maintenance:** The commit history is mostly Fatih Arslan plus Dependabot. Most other contributors have 1–5 commits each, so they look like one-off contributors, not co-maintainers.
- **Possible duplicates:** Some blame names may be the same person under different names, such as `Greg Poirson` and `greg`. That would shift the percentages only slightly.

So the code isn't owned by one person, but the maintenance is. Day-to-day work and review appear to come from Fatih Arslan alone. This is a small library of about 2,000 lines, so forking it would be cheap if the maintainer stopped working on it.

I didn't check how recently the commits were made or who has push access on GitHub. Those would tell you more about maintainer risk than line counts do. I can pull the recent commit dates per author if you want them.
